stock buy-backs.

00:00:32.132 --> 00:00:34.434 size:84% position:42%
You don't have a lot of
business investment.

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:35.135 line:92%
The fact of the matter is

00:00:35.135 --> 00:00:39.339
economic development continues
to be intensely concentrated

00:00:39.339 --> 00:00:41.508 align:start size:94% position:6%
in a relatively small
geographic area.

00:00:41.508 --> 00:00:44.911
That was the whole critiqu
against the Obama economy

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:48.782
back in 2016 when Donald Trump
sort of banged on about that

00:00:48.782 --> 00:00:49.816 size:81% position:41% line:92%
about bad trade deals.

00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:51.885 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's a GM plant
that closed this week

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:54.054 align:start size:94% position:6%
10 miles from the debate,
in Warren.

00:00:54.054 --> 00:00:56.156 align:start size:94% position:6%
In Lordstown, Ohio,
Donald Trump --

00:00:56.156 --> 00:00:57.323 line:92%
you should watch this clip -

00:00:57.323 --> 00:01:00.293
he gets up in front of Lordstown
and he says to them,

00:01:00.293 --> 00:01:02.095 align:start size:94% position:6%
"This plant's
not going anywhere.

00:01:02.095 --> 00:01:03.129 size:81% position:41% line:92%
Don't sell your home."

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:05.765 size:78% position:39%
Don't sell your home,
don't do anything.

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:06.900 line:92%
They shut the plant down.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.134 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, the same
structural factors,

00:01:08.134 --> 00:01:11.137
and then you put on top of tha
some of the trade war stuff,

00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:12.772 align:start size:94% position:6%
which has really hammered
a lot of farmers,

00:01:12.772 --> 00:01:15.241
I think there's a case to be
made about why the Trump economy

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:17.811
is not as good as it looks
for a lot of working people,

00:01:17.811 --> 00:01:20.547
and I would like to see
Democrats make that case.

00:01:20.547 --> 00:01:22.582 size:78% position:39% line:92%
-And why do you think

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:24.684 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's not something
they've picked up on yet?

00:01:24.684 --> 00:01:26.686 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I think you see them
try to start to make it,

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:29.589 align:start size:94% position:6%
but what ends up happening
in these debates, particularly

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:31.224 align:start size:94% position:6%
is they're competing with
each other,

00:01:31.224 --> 00:01:33.860 align:start size:94% position:6%
so they're focused on area
where they have areas

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:35.528 align:start size:94% position:6%
of agreement or disagreement
or a tax.

00:01:35.528 --> 00:01:38.264 align:start size:94% position:6%
I do think that Bernie Sanders
and Elizabeth Warren

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:39.332 line:92%
and Joe Biden, to some extent,

00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:41.534 size:88% position:44%
when Biden talked about
the auto rescue tonight,

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:42.602 line:92%
when he was asked the question

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:43.603 align:start size:94% position:6%
"How are you gonna
win Michigan?"

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:46.039
He talked about the auto rescue,
which is a good thing to do,

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:48.441 align:start size:94% position:6%
because the auto rescue wa
amazingly important

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:50.243 align:start size:94% position:6%
and effective piece of
policy intervention

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:54.013
that saved tens of thousands o
jobs across the greater Midwest.

00:01:54.013 --> 00:01:56.716 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Climate change came up tonigh
as well.

00:01:56.716 --> 00:01:59.552 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is an issue you're gonn
do a special on.

00:01:59.552 --> 00:02:01.020 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, we're doing
a two-day forum

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.388 align:start size:94% position:6%
with as many candidates
as we can get.

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:06.993 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I imagine preparing for tha
must be difficult in that

00:02:06.993 --> 00:02:08.895 size:81% position:41%
the breadth of things
you have to talk about

00:02:08.895 --> 00:02:11.197
when you're talking about
the climate is so vast.

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:13.266 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It is. It's a hard thing
to zero in on.

00:02:13.266 --> 00:02:16.369 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's also hard to
sort of move between

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:17.871 align:start size:94% position:6%
the specialized vocabulary o
people

00:02:17.871 --> 00:02:20.907
that are in the world of climate
policy or climate activism

00:02:20.907 --> 00:02:22.675 align:start size:94% position:6%
and what the broader publi
knows.

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:24.277 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I think
it takes a lot of work

00:02:24.277 --> 00:02:26.212
to get really good conversations
going on

00:02:26.212 --> 00:02:27.747 align:start size:94% position:6%
that a lot of times
also politicians

00:02:27.747 --> 00:02:30.183 align:start size:94% position:6%
just don't know that much abou
it, frankly.

00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:31.417 line:92%
They know the briefing book.

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:32.252 line:92%
Jay Inslee is an exception

00:02:32.252 --> 00:02:34.487
I did a podcast with him,
why is this happening,

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:36.890 align:start size:94% position:6%
where he talked for an hou
about this stuff.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:38.224 line:92%
This is what he's running on

00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:40.593 line:92%
But a lot of them can't ge

00:02:40.593 --> 00:02:42.729 align:start size:94% position:6%
too far out past
their briefing book on it.

00:02:42.729 --> 00:02:45.398
That's not the case in something
like, for instance,

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:48.001
criminal justice reform,
immigration, or healthcare

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:51.371 align:start size:94% position:6%
All are issues I feel
that the majority of candidate

00:02:51.371 --> 00:02:52.205 size:84% position:42% line:92%
have more facility with

00:02:52.205 --> 00:02:53.873 align:start size:94% position:6%
than at this point
they do climate policy.

00:02:53.873 --> 00:02:57.177 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And do you think part of that
too is just the massivenes

00:02:57.177 --> 00:03:00.346 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the undertaking to fix
where the climate is heading

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:02.749 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Did you hear the Andrew Yan
answer tonight?

00:03:02.749 --> 00:03:04.384 align:start size:94% position:6%
Andrew Yang
was asked about climate,

00:03:04.384 --> 00:03:06.386 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he said, "Oh, probably not
gonna fix it.

00:03:06.386 --> 00:03:09.289 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're gonna need to move
your family to higher ground

00:03:09.289 --> 00:03:12.592 align:start size:94% position:6%
so we're giving you
$1,000 a month."

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:13.526 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:14.460 size:75% position:38% line:92%
That's bleak, buddy.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:15.361 line:92%
-That was real bleak, yeah

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:19.732
He had some nice moments tonight
but that was a tough one.

00:03:19.732 --> 00:03:20.300 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-It was bleak.

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:21.968 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't think $1,000 a month i
going to do it,

00:03:21.968 --> 00:03:22.902 align:start size:94% position:6%
if you need to go
to higher ground.

00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:26.206
But what I did think about tha
is it did puncture a little bi

00:03:26.206 --> 00:03:28.608 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the kind of like,
"We can do it, we can do it.

00:03:28.608 --> 00:03:32.478
Being clear-eyed about wha
we're up against right now

00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:33.146 size:75% position:38% line:92%
is really important,

00:03:33.146 --> 00:03:35.548 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it's very hard to do tha
and balance that

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:38.384 align:start size:94% position:6%
with the kind of hopefulness
that is necessary

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:39.385 line:92%
to kind of motivate people

00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:42.021 align:start size:94% position:6%
and sort of put together
a coalition you need.

00:03:42.021 --> 00:03:44.057 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You've never moderated
a debate.

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:44.691 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
You would like to.

00:03:44.691 --> 00:03:46.826 align:start size:94% position:6%
It seems to be
an impossible undertaking.

00:03:46.826 --> 00:03:49.696
-It is an impossible job
that I would love to have.

00:03:49.696 --> 00:03:51.698
I mean, really, it is impossible
I know.

00:03:51.698 --> 00:03:54.701 align:start size:94% position:6%
You watch a debate,
you watch people on Twitter,

00:03:54.701 --> 00:03:55.835 line:92%
or you talk to family member

00:03:55.835 --> 00:03:58.638
who are very critical of
how the debate is managed,

00:03:58.638 --> 00:04:01.140 align:start size:94% position:6%
which just goes with
the territory.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:01.674 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Of course.

00:04:01.674 --> 00:04:03.676 align:start size:94% position:6%
-With that many people,
you got 10 people on stage

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:08.982 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're trying to be fair
in distributing talking time

00:04:08.982 --> 00:04:10.083 size:81% position:41% line:92%
Politicians talk long.

00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:12.785 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's hard to get
fruitful exchanges.

00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:14.053 line:92%
It's hard to cut people off.

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:14.887 line:92%
And then when things go long

00:04:14.887 --> 00:04:17.924
I saw people complaining about
how long the CNN debate went

00:04:17.924 --> 00:04:19.892 align:start size:94% position:6%
but it's like,
it's either gonna go long

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:22.061 align:start size:94% position:6%
or they're gonna cut them off,
right?

00:04:22.061 --> 00:04:22.829 size:84% position:42% line:92%
Or you do fewer topics.

00:04:22.829 --> 00:04:24.530 align:start size:94% position:6%
If you do fewer topics,
then people say,

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:27.200 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Oh, you didn't get this topic
this topic, this topic."

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:28.301 line:92%
So a huge part of the challenge,

00:04:28.301 --> 00:04:30.169 align:start size:94% position:6%
particularly at this point
in the cycle,

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:32.305 align:start size:94% position:6%
is just managing
that many people on stage.

00:04:32.305 --> 00:04:34.207 align:start size:94% position:6%
You've got
a choir's worth of people

00:04:34.207 --> 00:04:34.974 line:92%
that you got to deal with.

00:04:34.974 --> 00:04:37.710 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think later on if you've got
four or five people,

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:38.411 size:81% position:41% line:92%
you can get something.

00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:40.079 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And that will happen one day,
right?

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:41.581 line:92%
It'll get down to four or five

00:04:41.581 --> 00:04:42.582 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:42.582 --> 00:04:43.983 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes, I think so.
-Okay, good.

00:04:43.983 --> 00:04:46.986
-What's really interesting to me
is what's the over/under o

00:04:46.986 --> 00:04:48.454 line:92%
the number of people still in it

00:04:48.454 --> 00:04:51.891 align:start size:94% position:6%
by February 6th or wheneve
the Iowa caucus is.

00:04:51.891 --> 00:04:54.327 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah. Eight.
Over/under is eight.

00:04:54.327 --> 00:04:56.829
-I think somewhere around 10
probably is probably the answer.

00:04:56.829 --> 00:04:58.331 size:81% position:41%
-Well, come back then.
Let's talk about it.

00:04:58.331 --> 00:05:01.301 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thanks so much for coming here
in the middle of the night

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:02.835 size:84% position:42% line:92%
Chris Hayes, everybody.

